WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.945
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.945 --> 00:00:10.862
Hi, I'm Ken Shotts, I'm a professor of
political economy at the Stanford GSB.

00:00:10.862 --> 00:00:12.546
&gt;&gt; My name is Neil Malhotra,

00:00:12.546 --> 00:00:16.689
I'm also a professor of political
economy at the Stanford GSB.

00:00:16.689 --> 00:00:21.341
And Ken and I would like to share some key
class takeaways from the course we teach,

00:00:21.341 --> 00:00:25.265
Leading With Values, which is also
the title of our forthcoming book

00:00:25.265 --> 00:00:28.705
[MUSIC]

00:00:28.705 --> 00:00:32.473
When people disagree about ethical
issues in the workplace or

00:00:32.473 --> 00:00:37.183
in their organizations, sometimes these
disagreements are about facts and

00:00:37.183 --> 00:00:39.370
sometimes they're about values.

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:44.070
Now when we disagree over facts,
these are not easy things to resolve, but

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:48.114
they can be addressed if people
can bring evidence to the table.

00:00:48.114 --> 00:00:50.121
This is not the case with values.

00:00:50.121 --> 00:00:54.890
No amount of evidence is going to convince
people that their values are right or

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:55.406
wrong.

00:00:55.406 --> 00:00:57.451
So where do these values come from?

00:00:57.451 --> 00:01:01.849
Well, psychologists have shown that
they come from deep seated moral

00:01:01.849 --> 00:01:06.256
intuitions that lie in our gut, and
they often have a cultural basis.

00:01:06.256 --> 00:01:09.137
What does this mean for
leading with values?

00:01:09.137 --> 00:01:13.851
Well, when people disagree about
ethical issues in the workplace,

00:01:13.851 --> 00:01:18.077
it's not because some people are moral and
some are immoral,

00:01:18.077 --> 00:01:23.949
is that they're bringing different core
values based considerations to the table.

00:01:23.949 --> 00:01:24.521
[MUSIC]

00:01:24.521 --> 00:01:28.609
People around you, specifically
those who are critical of you and

00:01:28.609 --> 00:01:32.772
your company, oftentimes are motivated
by key moral intuitions.

00:01:32.772 --> 00:01:37.237
So it's not helpful to just dismiss their
concerns out of hand by saying that

00:01:37.237 --> 00:01:40.900
they're self-interested or
motivated by money.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:44.782
It's oftentimes important to respect
the fact that they have moral intuitions,

00:01:44.782 --> 00:01:47.520
even if they're different from your own.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:51.300
We like to oftentimes think of
ourselves as very rational and

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:55.314
everybody else as driven by emotion,
but this isn't the case.

00:01:55.314 --> 00:02:00.426
Even when we're making very rigorous,
highly structured analytical arguments,

00:02:00.426 --> 00:02:04.120
the roots of those arguments
are in our gut intuitions.

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:07.361
And that self-awareness is
important to be reflective and

00:02:07.361 --> 00:02:08.990
to be skeptical of yourself.

00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:09.639
[MUSIC]

00:02:09.639 --> 00:02:14.193
If you want to communicate with others,
persuade them, and

00:02:14.193 --> 00:02:19.823
manage disagreements in your organization,
it's oftentimes important

00:02:19.823 --> 00:02:25.473
not to use your own moral intuitions
as the basis of framing your argument.

00:02:25.473 --> 00:02:30.268
But to be empathetic, and to see what
are other people's moral intuitions, and

00:02:30.268 --> 00:02:33.582
construct your arguments
about how they see the world,

00:02:33.582 --> 00:02:36.209
not necessarily just
how you see the world.

00:02:36.209 --> 00:02:39.583
[MUSIC]

00:02:39.583 --> 00:02:42.779
&gt;&gt; Our students talk a lot about
their sense of purpose, and

00:02:42.779 --> 00:02:46.585
their personal values, and
their personal ethical commitments.

00:02:46.585 --> 00:02:51.456
We also see leaders of companies talking
about how they want all of their employees

00:02:51.456 --> 00:02:52.868
to do the right thing and

00:02:52.868 --> 00:02:57.430
to behave in ways that are aligned
with the company's values.

00:02:57.430 --> 00:03:02.502
And at a societal level, we see many
companies, especially nowadays, talking

00:03:02.502 --> 00:03:08.010
about their commitments to improving
society in a whole lot of different ways.

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:11.426
All of these good
intentions are wonderful.

00:03:11.426 --> 00:03:18.183
However, as wonderful as good intentions
are, good institutions are even better.

00:03:18.183 --> 00:03:21.460
So what do we mean by good institutions?

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:26.729
We mean setting up incentives, rules,
and norms that promote good behavior.

00:03:26.729 --> 00:03:28.936
So this is most obvious within a company.

00:03:28.936 --> 00:03:33.439
When a leader of a company says, we expect
our employees to do the right thing,

00:03:33.439 --> 00:03:36.933
here's our values as a company,
that's good intentions.

00:03:36.933 --> 00:03:42.070
Good institutions is setting up incentives
and rules and norms within the company so

00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:47.226
that people are rewarded for doing things
that are aligned with that good behavior.

00:03:47.226 --> 00:03:52.970
And are not rewarded for doing things
that are out of step with those values.

00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:53.639
[MUSIC]
And

00:03:53.639 --> 00:03:57.109
if we want companies to do
the right thing for society,

00:03:57.109 --> 00:04:02.165
when push comes to shove, we need to
have government regulations, rules, and

00:04:02.165 --> 00:04:07.158
laws that incentivize companies to do
those things that are good for society.

00:04:07.158 --> 00:04:11.990
Rather than giving them perverse
incentives to do things that might

00:04:11.990 --> 00:04:13.437
be bad for society.

00:04:13.437 --> 00:04:17.135
That set of government rules,
laws, and institutions,

00:04:17.135 --> 00:04:22.039
that's really what's crucial for
unleashing the amazingly productive and

00:04:22.039 --> 00:04:27.254
innovative capacities of capitalism in
ways that are good for society as a whole.

00:04:27.254 --> 00:04:30.693
[MUSIC]

00:04:30.693 --> 00:04:37.164
I have a 13 year old daughter,

00:04:37.164 --> 00:04:45.803
and I think she's entirely unimpressed.

00:04:45.803 --> 00:04:49.590
So I think from her perspective,
I don't know anything about anything.

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:53.375
&gt;&gt; I think my daughter and
my parents would have the same reaction,

00:04:53.375 --> 00:04:57.456
which is my wife is the real scientist and
I'm the social scientist.

00:04:57.456 --> 00:05:06.178
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

